dns-parallel-lookups: The dns client tools (dnsip and friends) ask every
name and every @server one after another, because dnsopt_run calls the
lookup function in a plain loop. With several servers the slowest one holds
up all the others, and a dead server costs its full timeout each time.

Doing the lookups side by side needs changes in the library, which is why it
was left out. dns_resolve and dns_ip4 use the single global dns_resolve_tx
and a blocking loop around iopause, and dns_resolvconfip returns one
process-wide server list that dnsopt switches through the environment. The
plan is this:

  - Give dns_ip4, dns_ip6, dns_mx, dns_txt and dns_name a variant that takes
    its own struct dns_transmit and server list, with a start function and a
    step function that finishes without blocking, like dns_transmit_start,
    dns_transmit_io and dns_transmit_get already do.
  - Let dnsopt_run start one transmit per name and server pair, collect the
    iopause_fd entries into one array, call iopause once and step each
    transmit that is ready.
  - Print the results in command line order, not in arrival order, so the
    server prefix lines stay grouped per name.

The pieces to touch are lib/dns/dns_resolve.c, dns_ip4.c, dns_ip6.c,
dns_mx.c, dns_txt.c, dns_name.c and dnsopt.c.

terminal-windows-untested: The terminal module is only compile checked
for a native Windows console, with mingw. Nothing of it has run there yet,
neither the output nor terminal_getkey.

It needs a run on a real console that checks text with UTF-8, colors and
styles, the erase and scroll calls, the alternate screen, cursor save and
restore, and the keys: arrows, page keys, function keys with modifiers, and
the resize event. least itself does not build for Windows yet, so a small
test program that calls the terminal functions is the quickest way.

On POSIX a size change is still signalled by SIGWINCH and handled by the
program. A terminal_resize hook that installs the handler and reports it as
TERMINAL_KEY_RESIZE through a self-pipe would make both systems look the
same.


- genmakefile-compound-literals: The compiler setup in src/genmakefile/
  compiler.c builds its flag lists with a series of var_push calls. The
  predefined macros of a compiler in project.c, bcc55_defs, show a tidier
  way: a plain list of strings that the code walks over.

  Where such a list is used in only one place, it need not be a named
  variable at all. A compound literal at the point of use, for example a
  (const char* const[]){...} passed to a small helper that pushes every
  entry, keeps the data next to the code that needs it. Rework the
  per-compiler branches of set_compiler_type this way, and let the table of
  bcc55 macros move into the same place. Not started; the move of the
  compiler code into compiler.c was done as it was.

- genmakefile-scan-diagnostics: The preprocessor scan of the sources
  prints what lib/cpp reports for a unit: warnings about #warning lines,
  "extra token" at an #endif, and errors such as "expected ')'" at lines
  that are plain code. A run over src/ prints several hundred such lines,
  which hides what -v tells.

  In scan_only mode the diagnostics of the preprocessor should be collected
  and shown only when -v is given twice, with a count at -v. The errors on
  plain code lines (src/genmakefile/project.c:254 is one) need a look of
  their own, since a scan should not parse code at all.
